{{smaller block|<poem>Plight me the full ''assurance'' of your faith.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', T. N., iv. 3.}}}}
'''2.''' Pledge; guaranty; surety.
{{smaller block|You should procure him better ''assurance'' than Bardolph; he would not take his bond and yours; he liked not the security.
{{right|''Shak.'', 2 Hen. IV., i. 2.}}}}
'''3†.''' Affiance; betrothal.
{{smaller block|The day of their ''assurance'' drew nigh.
{{right|''Sir P. Sidney'', Arcadia.}}
<poem>I am sure
I never courted you, nor gave you tokens
That might concern ''assurance''.</poem>
{{right|''Beau. and Fl.'', Coxcomb, iii. 1.}}}}
'''4.''' In ''law'', documentary evidence of the title or right of possession of property.—'''5.''' Insurance; a contract for the payment of a sum on the occurrence of a certain event, as loss or death.
{{smaller block|Recent writers have sought to establish distinctions of a novel character between them [''assurance'' and ''insurance'']. One of these is that a person insures his life, his house, or his ships, and the office assures to him in each of these cases a sum of money payable in certain contingencies. Another is that ''assurance'' represents the principle and insurance the practice.
{{right|''Encyc. Brit.'', XIII. 169.}}}}
'''6.''' Certain proof; clear evidence; positive demonstration; undeniable grounds for belief or trust; assuredness.
{{smaller block|Whereof he hath given ''assurance'' unto all men, in that he hath raised him from the dead.
{{right|Acts xvii. 31.}}
<poem>A form, indeed,
Where every god did seem to set his seal,
To give the world ''assurance'' of a man.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Hamlet, iii. 4.}}
<poem>I feel desires
That give ''assurance'' of their own success,
And that, infus’d from Heav’n, must thither tend.</poem>}}

readings.c1s2:
{{smaller block|{{right|''Cowper'', The Task, v.}}
A brightness, like that of the eyes of some smaller animals, which gives ''assurance'' of life, but of a life foreign and unintelligible. {{right|''Lowell'', Study Windows, p. 44.}}}}
'''7.''' Firm persuasion; full confidence or trust; freedom from doubt; certain expectation; the utmost certainty.
{{smaller block|Let us draw near with a true heart, in full ''assurance'' of faith. {{right|Heb. x. 22.}}
<poem>I’ll make ''assurance'' double sure,
And take a bond of fate. {{right|''Shak.'', Macbeth, iv. 1.}}</poem>
There have prevailed very widely . . . among mankind the sad tradition of a lost or forfeited life of perfection and happiness, and a dim expectation or the firm ''assurance'' of a future life of perfection and happiness. {{right|''Maudsley'', Body and Will, p. 197.}}}}
Especially—'''8.''' Firmness of mind; undoubting steadiness; intrepidity; courage.
{{smaller block|Brave men meet danger with ''assurance''. {{right|''Knolles''.}}
He is wanting in neither personal courage, ''assurance'', nor promptitude, but he abuses these virtues by using them in the service of vice. {{right|''P. Robinson'', Under the Sun, p. 31.}}}}
'''9.''' Freedom from timidity or bashfulness; laudable confidence; self-reliance.
{{smaller block|Conversation with the world will give them knowledge and ''assurance''. {{right|''Locke''.}}
I have been often surprised that you, who have seen so much of the world, . . . could never yet acquire a requisite share of ''assurance''. {{right|''Goldsmith'', She Stoops to Conquer, ii.}}}}
'''10.''' Excess of boldness; impudence: as, his ''assurance'' is intolerable.
{{smaller block|Immoderate ''assurance'' is perfect licentiousness. {{right|''Shenstone''.}}}}

